      "stream_token_id_count": 512,
      "text": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a technical guide for adding a new benchmark to an existing optimization repo. This is a practical, structured document. Let me think about what makes a good optimization benchmark repo structure and what the key concerns are:\n\n1. Directory structure - where scripts, data, notes, patches, results go\n2. Naming conventions for run directories\n3. Reproducibility requirements\n4. Validation criteria for promoting results\n\nLet me think about what a real optimization repo looks like. Think of repos like those for compiler optimization, algorithm optimization, numerical methods optimization. They typically have:\n- A top-level structure with benchmarks organized by problem\n- Each benchmark has its own directory\n- Scripts for running, data files, patches that implement the optimization, notes documenting the approach, and results\n\nLet me structure this as a practical guide with clear sections. I'll make it concrete with examples rather than abstract. The tone should be direct, technical, no fluff. Like a CONTRIBUTING.md or internal wiki page.\n\nLet me think about the specific sections:\n- Directory layout (where things go)\n- Naming conventions (run directories, files)\n- What to record (reproducibility)\n- Validation/promotion criteria\n\nI'll use a concrete example benchmark to ground the advice. Let me pick something like \"sparse matrix multiply\" or \"FFT\" or \"graph shortest path\" - something that feels real. I'll use a generic but concrete example.\n\nLet me write this as a clean, well-structured guide. Markdown formatting since it's a technical document.\n</think>\n\n# Adding a New Benchmark to the Optimization Repo\n\n## 1. Directory Layout\n\nEvery benchmark lives under `benchmarks/<name>/`. Create the skeleton before writing any code:\n\n```\nbenchmarks/\n  <name>/\n    README.md          # one-paragraph problem statement + how to run\n    scripts/\n      run.sh           # single entry point; orchestrates everything\n      setup.sh         # fetches/builds data, installs deps\n      collect.py       # parses raw output \u2192 structured summary\n    data/\n      input/           # fixed test inputs (committed or fetched)\n      expected/        # reference outputs for correctness checks\n    patches/\n      baseline.diff    # the \"before\" state (or a pointer to a commit)\n      <approach>.diff  # one file per optimization attempt\n    notes/\n      <date>-<slug>.md # design notes",
      "text_preview": "\n\n<think>\nThe user wants a technical guide for adding a new benchmark to an existing optimization repo. This is a practical, structured document. Let me think about what makes a good optimization benchmark repo structure and what the key concerns are:\n\n1. Directory structure - where scripts, data, notes, patches, resul",
